    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d131-a1">

                <pb facs="0188" n="180" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg180"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Annex]<anchor n="10" xml:id="d131-f1"/></seg>
                </opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Draft of a Telegram to the Ambassador in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d131-f2"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-08-05">August 5, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The President has considered the proposal made in your 3365, August 1, 11 p.m.
                    From your telegram it is assumed that the primary question is a recognition by
                    the United States of the special position of Australia and New Zealand in the
                    Far East, and that you have in mind the suggestion of a plan by which (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">a</hi>) India is raised to Dominion status; (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) India, Australia, New Zealand, and China enter
                    into a defensive alliance; (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>) that the United
                    States indicates in an appropriate manner that it would be prepared to give
                    assistance to such an alliance.</p>
                <p>Your suggestion is further understood to mean that we proceed towards this plan
                    in steps, first proposing the raising of India to <pb facs="0189" n="181"
                        xml:id="pg181"/> Dominion status, and thereafter working out the
                    relationship between the four powers in question.</p>
                <p>The President and the Department believe that the time is favorable for proposing
                    such a plan, and authorizes you to present it to the Prime Minister and to the
                    Foreign Office.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
